        <!-- SECTION 4: Kit vs. Components -->
        <div id="kit-vs-components" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. DIY Kit vs. Buying Components Separately
          </h2>
          <p>
            One question experienced DIYers often wrestle with is whether to buy a prepackaged kit or source the individual components — etching solution, base coat, topcoat, and chips — separately from specialty suppliers. Both approaches have genuine merits, and the right choice depends on your project scale, experience level, and the specific floor conditions you're working with.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When an All-in-One Kit Makes Sense</h3>
          <p>
            For most homeowners coating a residential garage floor for the first time, a complete kit is almost always the better choice. The components in a quality kit are formulated to work together — the etching solution is matched to the epoxy chemistry, the base coat and topcoat are compatible, and the included chips are appropriately sized for the product's application window. Beyond compatibility, kits eliminate the research burden of sourcing compatible components, calculating quantities across multiple products, and managing multiple shipping orders or store trips. When something goes wrong during a kit application, the manufacturer's customer service line can address the problem as a complete system. That kind of support disappears when you've assembled components from multiple suppliers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Kits also deliver better value for single-floor projects. Specialty epoxy suppliers often sell base coats, topcoats, and etching chemicals in quantities significantly larger than a single garage floor requires. Unless you have multiple projects planned, buying standard kit sizes avoids wasting expensive leftover product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Sourcing Components Separately Makes Sense</h3>
          <p>
            Building a custom system from individual components makes sense in a few specific scenarios. If you have a very large floor — a three-car garage, a workshop, a commercial bay — buying commercial quantities of each component from a specialty supplier often delivers better pricing per square foot than purchasing multiple consumer kits. Commercial-grade products from specialty suppliers frequently offer higher solids content, better chemical resistance, and more color options than consumer kits from hardware stores.
          </p>
          <p>
            Custom systems also make sense when you have specific performance requirements that no single kit addresses. For example, a workshop floor that needs maximum chemical resistance might benefit from a 100% solids epoxy base coat paired with a polyaspartic topcoat — a combination that no standard consumer kit offers as a package. Similarly, if your concrete has specific issues (extreme porosity, previous coating failures, or active moisture migration), you may need products — concrete densifiers, moisture-blocking primers, or specialty adhesion promoters — that don't fit into the component list of a standard kit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">The Simple Rule</p>
            <p class="text-amber-900 text-sm leading-relaxed">If this is your first epoxy project or you're coating a single residential garage, buy a complete kit. If you have multiple floors, commercial-scale square footage, or specific performance requirements no kit addresses, source components from a specialty supplier and build a custom system.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Coverage Calculator -->
        <div id="coverage-calculator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Epoxy Do You Need?
          </h2>
          <p>
            One of the most common epoxy project mistakes is underestimating how much product is needed and running out mid-application — a scenario that creates visible lap lines and dry spots that compromise both appearance and performance. Calculating your coverage needs before you order eliminates this risk entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standard Garage Floor Square Footage</h3>
          <p>
            A standard one-car garage bay measures approximately <strong>200–250 square feet</strong> of floor space. A typical two-car attached garage runs <strong>440–500 square feet</strong>. A three-car garage reaches <strong>600–800 square feet</strong> depending on the layout. These are gross floor areas — the actual coatable surface after subtracting any fixed obstructions (built-in workbenches, floor drains, permanent storage platforms) will be somewhat less.
          </p>
          <p>
            When calculating the product quantity needed, always work from your actual measured square footage, not a rough estimate. Measure the length and width of the garage bay, multiply them together, and then subtract the area of any built-in features. This floor area in square feet is your coverage target.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 25% Buffer Rule</h3>
          <p>
            As discussed earlier, label coverage ratings assume ideal smooth concrete conditions. Real garage floors are rougher and more porous and will absorb more product than the stated coverage assumes. After calculating your floor area, add a <strong>25% buffer</strong> to account for real-world conditions. For a 440-square-foot two-car garage, that means targeting at least 550 square feet of product coverage in your purchase — which typically means buying two standard two-car kits or one commercial-quantity order.
          </p>
          <p>
            Additionally, factor in a second coat if you intend to apply one. Most quality epoxy systems benefit from a second coat on porous or rough concrete, and the topcoat — whether included in the kit or purchased separately — adds another full-floor coverage requirement to your product list. Calculate every coat in your coverage planning before you place your order.
          </p>

