        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What's typically included in an epoxy garage floor kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A complete epoxy garage floor kit should include: an etching solution (acid concentrate) to prepare the concrete surface, Part A resin and Part B hardener for the epoxy base coat, decorative vinyl chips to broadcast into the wet surface, and ideally a clear topcoat or second coat of epoxy. Lower-cost kits may omit the etching solution or topcoat, requiring you to source those items separately. Always check the kit contents before purchasing to understand what additional products you may need to complete the project properly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to apply an epoxy garage floor kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Plan for a <strong>two-day project minimum</strong> for a complete installation. Day one covers surface preparation: degreasing the floor, applying the etching solution, rinsing thoroughly, and allowing the concrete to dry completely (12–24 hours). Day two covers application: mixing and applying the base coat, broadcasting decorative chips, and allowing the base coat to cure. If your kit includes a topcoat, apply it 12–24 hours after the base coat. Expect an additional 72 hours before vehicle parking for water-based systems, or 24–36 hours for polycuramine products.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply an epoxy kit over old epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">It depends on the condition of the existing coating. If the old epoxy is fully adhered, clean, and not peeling, you can apply a new coat of epoxy over it — but you must first scuff-sand the entire surface with 80-grit sandpaper to create mechanical adhesion for the new coat. If the existing epoxy is peeling, delaminating, or was applied over a contaminated surface, it must be removed entirely by grinding before recoating. Never apply new epoxy over a failing or partially delaminated existing coating — the new coat will fail in the same locations. When in doubt, peel a corner: if the old coating lifts easily, remove it all before proceeding.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you don't etch the concrete before applying epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">The coating will almost certainly fail — it's not a question of whether but when. Smooth, unsealed concrete has almost no surface porosity for the epoxy to mechanically bond into. Acid etching (or mechanical grinding) opens the concrete's surface pores and creates a profile that the epoxy can grip. Without this preparation, the epoxy adhesion relies entirely on chemistry against a smooth surface, which produces adhesion strength that is a fraction of what properly prepared concrete achieves. Under the thermal cycling from hot and cold days and the stress of vehicle parking, this weak bond fails — typically within weeks to months, manifesting as large sheets of epoxy peeling away from the floor. Etching is not optional: it is the single most important step in the entire installation process.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

