        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Epoxy Garage Floor Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy is unforgiving in a way most DIY coatings are not: once the two parts are mixed, the clock runs whether you are ready or not, and the slab has to have been prepared long before that.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Moisture Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vapour rising through a slab is the single most common cause of epoxy delaminating in sheets, and it is invisible until it happens. Tape a two-foot square of plastic sheet down for 24 to 48 hours; if the underside is damp, you need a moisture-tolerant primer or a different system entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Etching Instead of Grinding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid etching is the kit instruction because it is cheap, not because it is best. On a hard-troweled or previously sealed slab it barely opens the surface, and the epoxy peels a year later. Renting a diamond grinder for a day is the single biggest upgrade to a DIY epoxy floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing More Than You Can Spread</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two-part epoxy has a pot life measured in tens of minutes, and it accelerates as it sits in the bucket. Mixing the whole kit at once means the last third cures in the pail. Mix one batch at a time, spread it immediately, and have a second person ready to mix the next.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Oil Stains</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy bonds to concrete, not to oil, and any contaminated spot becomes a fisheye or a bare patch. Degrease every stain thoroughly and let the area dry, and treat deep-set stains with a poultice before you start. A slab that looks clean when dry can still be saturated where a car sat for years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

